Subject: [PATCH 3/3] docs: add the h20/h50 horizon rows and withdraw the "no
 real-time horizon can see it" claim

h50 completes (169.0 s, delta-v 2.549, zero violations), which contradicts the
reading the h10 row alone suggested. It does not crawl in co-rotating: it drops
from 16.4 m to 7.5 m in 45 s with target-frame speed held at 0.15-0.35, then
synchronises. The lookahead the cheap strategy needs is between 1 s and 5 s,
not the 119 s the oracle's coast happens to take.

The measured frontier on seed 262000, serial, one process:

  oracle  complete 228.5 s  dv 1.512   --
  h10     fails, 4.516 m short, dv 5.074, 41.3/50.2 ms, 0.41x/0.50x budget
  h20     fails, 0.616 m short, dv 3.895, 80.2/93.2 ms, 0.80x/0.93x budget
  h50     complete 169.0 s   dv 2.549, 191.7/217.2 ms, 1.92x/2.17x budget

Two holes this row set has to survive before it is a defect at all, both now
written into the handoff: h20 given 400 s parks at 0.264 m against a 0.25 m
tolerance, which is a steady-state offset a cost-weight change may erase, and
h50 clears that tolerance by 0.004 m; and reference_source="fixed" gives the
optimiser no path plan outside the latch, which is exactly the unfair
comparison CLAUDE.md trap 10 warns about. The Go/No-Go is therefore not passed:
an input/terminal-weight sensitivity sweep at h20 comes first.

